FujiNet Go CoCo (desktop) -- what is done and what is next
==========================================================

The port follows fujinet-go-adam-desktop/PORTING.md, whose §12 milestone
order this file tracks. The derivation is

    coco-desktop = (adam-desktop - adam-android) + coco-android

with the Apple II port as the closer model (it already solved variable frame
geometry, analog axes and the ROM-embedding gate).


Done (this pass) -- M1, M2, M3
------------------------------

Build system
  * cmake/Dependencies.cmake -- coco_provide_dependency() with a new
    ARCHIVE/SHA256 branch, because XRoar ships a release tarball rather than a
    git repository. XRoar 1.11 and fujinet-firmware 07e1bed30 are pinned.
  * cmake/StageXRoar.cmake -- stages src/ + portalib/ + top-config.h into
    core/xroar-generated, swaps in the lazy-connect becker.c, and runs
    tools/xroar/patch-staged-tree.py. Re-stages when the source, the patch
    script or the staging recipe changes; -DXROAR_RESTAGE=ON forces it.
  * tools/xroar/patch-staged-tree.py -- generates config.h (headless
    CoCo/Dragon feature set, host half resolved by the preprocessor) and
    registers our ui/ao modules in src/ui.c and src/ao.c under
    XROAR_DESKTOP_MODULES.
  * cmake/FujiNetRuntime.cmake, cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake.
  * WITH_COCO_ROMS gate + tools/xroar/embed-roms.py + the five ROM images.

Core
  * core/include/cocosession.h -- the frontend contract.
  * core/xroar/coco_host.{c,h} -- the Android host, with android/log.h
    replaced by a log-sink seam and a frame-rate sink added (the Android app
    ignored XRoar's notify_frame_rate; the desktop pacer needs it). Plus
    cocohost_release_all_keys() for focus loss.
  * core/xroar/desktop/{ui,ao,joystick}_desktop.c -- the three XRoar modules.
    ui_desktop.c uses VO_RENDER_FMT_ARGB8 (the explicit base format, never the
    VO_RENDER_FMT_ARGB32 alias), so every frontend paints XRGB8888 with no
    per-pixel conversion.
  * core/src/session.c -- lifecycle and the paced emulator loop. FujiNet is
    started BEFORE the emulator, which is the opposite of ADAM and Apple II
    and follows from FujiNet being the one that listens here.
  * core/src/input_map.c -- keysym -> XRoar hkbd scancode (USB HID usage
    codes), positional via a US-layout unshift; and the 0..65535 analog axis
    mapping with 32767 at centre.
  * core/src/paths.c -- materialises the embedded ROMs into the user's ROM
    directory, skipping names already present so an import always wins.
  * core/src/gamepad_sdl.c -- reworked for the CoCo's TWO joystick ports
    (first pad -> right, second -> left, explicit binding available).
  * core/src/{settings,audio_sdl,fujinet_runtime,compat.h,dynlib.h}.c -- from
    the Apple II port with the target-specific parts adjusted.

Frontend
  * frontends/gnome -- GTK4/libadwaita. Machine / Display / Artifact Colour as
    live radio actions in the menu (the Display submenu is rebuilt on a machine
    switch, which is what removes RGB on a CoCo 2), preferences over the shared
    settings store, media and ROM import, FujiNet config + log windows.

Tests
  * input_test -- the pure mapping functions.
  * boot_smoke -- writes the embedded ROMs to a scratch directory, starts the
    core headless, runs 120 frames and checks geometry, non-black pixels and
    that XRoar reported a frame rate. Skips (77) on WITH_COCO_ROMS=OFF.
  * drivewire_smoke -- the end-to-end one. Starts a real session with the
    in-process runtime and asserts (a) an OP_READ line in FujiNet's own log,
    which can only appear if bytes crossed the loopback socket in BOTH
    directions, and (b) that typing changes the screen. Both bugs above were
    found by writing it; neither was visible from the outside. Skips (77) on
    no ROMs, no libfujinet, or a Becker port someone else is holding -- and
    says which, because a leftover standalone fujinet-pc-coco is the single
    most confusing way for this to look broken.
  * session_test -- media/ROM import routing, ROM detection per machine, the
    menu enumerations, the live setters' clamping, and settings persistence
    across sessions. No emulator, so it runs in milliseconds.
  * dasm_test -- the 6809 disassembler: one named case per encoding that is
    easy to get wrong (page prefixes, every indexed postbyte shape, PCR
    resolving against the END of the instruction, the push/pull register
    lists), plus a sweep over all 65536 two-byte openings asserting only that
    every one decodes to a usable length, so a disassembly list can never get
    stuck.
  * debug_test -- the debugger engine against a real running machine, which
    is the only way to test it: the design is that the emulator thread parks
    inside XRoar's instruction_hook, so a mock would only prove the mock
    works. Covers pause/step/breakpoints, that memory reads while parked are
    stable across two reads (i.e. the machine really is stopped, not just
    between frames), and that a session with the debugger PAUSED still tears
    down rather than deadlocking on the parked thread.
  * no_embedded_roms.py -- registered per shipped binary when
    WITH_COCO_ROMS=OFF.

M4 is done: settings, media import, gamepad port assignment and the audio
block-size contract are all covered. coco_pad_for_port is split out of
gamepad_sdl.c so the two-port assignment rules can be tested with no hardware
attached; writing those assertions found that it would happily assign a pad to
a third joystick port the CoCo does not have.

FujiNet runtime (M3)
  * tools/fujinet/build-fujinet-desktop.sh -- PC_TARGET=COCO, [BOIP] forced to
    127.0.0.1:65504. All eleven of the Apple II script's patch blocks are
    target-independent and applied unchanged; the COCO/DriveWire bus needs no
    transport transform, because the Becker port is already plain DriveWire
    over TCP. Built clean on the first attempt against fujinet-pc-coco
    b9a27cfa1, exporting all six ABI symbols.
  * tools/fujinet/support/fujinet_desktop_entry.cpp -- taken from the Apple II
    *working tree* rather than its last commit, for the stop-with-timeout fix
    (a device blocked on a socket call can miss the shutdown flag; detaching
    after 5s beats hanging the host process on exit).
  * Web admin on 64002, not the Android app's 8002: a standalone
    fujinet-pc-coco uses 8002. ADAM has 65214 and Apple II 64001, so all three
    desktop apps plus a standalone build can run at once.
  * fujinet_runtime.c sets FUJINET_RUNTIME_ROOT before starting the runtime.
    Two firmware patches root themselves from it and fall back to a relative
    path when unset -- which fails quietly (the CA store loads empty and every
    https:// fetch starts looking like a network fault). The Apple II port
    does not set it either; worth telling them.

  * fujinet_wait_for_becker() -- start is NOT enough. main_setup() returns as
    soon as the devices exist, but the DriveWire bus then logs "BoIPChannel:
    No WiFi!" and suspends itself for a FULL SECOND before listening. HDB-DOS
    boots in well under that, asks for its boot sector, gets nothing, and
    hangs on the banner with no OK prompt -- which looks exactly like a
    working emulator until you try to type. The session now probes the port
    until it accepts before starting the machine.
  * paths_init() resolves config/data/rom directories to absolute paths. The
    FujiNet entry wrapper chdir()s into its runtime root, and on this target
    FujiNet starts FIRST, so by the time XRoar resolves -rompath the CWD has
    moved and a relative path silently finds nothing: "(unpopulated)" ROM
    slots and a blank screen, with the files right there on disk.

Verified by hand
  * The CoCo 3 boots to its banner; the vsync phase lock engages at 60/60
    frames on vsync, 0 behind (COCO_PACE_LOG=1).
  * HDB-DOS reaches FujiNet's CONFIG over the Becker port, against both a
    standalone fujinet-pc-coco and the in-process runtime.
  * Clone-and-build both ways (M2): a fresh tree with no third_party/, and a
    `git archive` export with no git metadata at all. Both download the pinned
    XRoar tarball, verify its SHA256, stage it and test green.
  * Both ROM configurations: ctest green either way, and no_embedded_roms
    fails loudly on a WITH_COCO_ROMS=ON binary, as it should.


Next
----


M6 (in progress) The disassembler (6809 and 6309), the engine, both Linux
    debugger windows, their memory panes and the video pane are done. Live
    6309 machine selection and symbol tables are done too. Still open:
      * The macOS and Windows debugger windows, with those frontends (M10).

    Symbol tables: core/debugger/symtab.c (private; cocodebug_symbol_at()/
    cocodebug_symbol_find()/cocodebug_symbols_load()/cocodebug_symbols_
    clear() in cocodebug.h are the public surface). Two sources merged into
    one flat table, per the earlier "needs a source deciding first" note:
      * Built in: six Color BASIC ROM entry points ($A000-$A00A -- POLCAT,
        CHROUT, CSRDON, BLKIN, BLKOUT, JOYIN), sourced from Wikipedia's
        "Color BASIC" article (cross-checked against computerarcheology.com/
        CoCo/Hardware.html, which independently names the same six routines
        in the same range) and confirmed live against this project's own
        Super ECB ROM (core/tests: $A000 resolves to POLCAT, $A002 to
        CHROUT). HDB-DOS's and Super ECB's OWN additional entry points are
        deliberately NOT included: no equally authoritative primary source
        (a ROM disassembly or vendor doc, not a secondary summary) was in
        hand for those, and a wrong address in a debugger is worse than a
        missing one -- a good follow-up if someone has HDB-DOS's actual
        source or a verified disassembly.
      * User-loadable: LWASM's `--symbol-dump` format, verified against a
        real `lwasm --symbol-dump=file.sym` run rather than assumed --
        "NAME EQU $ADDR" per line. A plain "NAME ADDR" (hex with $/0x, or
        decimal) is also accepted for hand-written files. Loaded symbols
        shadow built-in ones at the same address.
    Both debugger windows get a disassembly annotation ("; NAME" appended to
    any line whose address has a symbol) and Load Symbols…/Clear Symbols
    toolbar buttons. core/tests/symtab_test.c covers the parser and lookup
    rules with no XRoar/ROM dependency at all.

    Live CPU switching: cocosession_set_cpu()/cocosession_cpu() (core/
    include/cocosession.h), COCOSESSION_CPU_MC6809/HD6309, wired through
    coco_host.c's cocohost_set_cpu() the same way the existing in-place
    CoCo2<->CoCo3 machine switch works -- mutate the running machine_
    config's cpu field, xroar_hard_reset() in place, no xroar re-init. Unlike
    the machine switch, this touches neither the TV input nor the cartridge
    ROM. -machine-cpu is also passed at initial xroar_init() (coco_host.c's
    cpu_name), so a session can boot straight into 6309 from a persisted
    setting rather than needing a switch after start. A CPU-only switch and
    an in-place machine switch queued in the same tick would otherwise race
    on which one's hard reset sees the new cpu value; g_current_cpu in
    coco_host.c is the fix, mirroring whichever was applied most recently so
    the machine-switch branch reapplies it to the newly selected config.
    Frontend menu items in both GNOME (win.cpu radio action) and KDE (the
    View > CPU submenu) list cocosession_cpu_name()'s two entries, matching
    the Machine/Display/Artifact Colour submenus' pattern. core/tests/
    cpu_switch_test.c boots a CoCo 3, switches live to 6309 and back,
    confirming both survive (the debugger's disassembler auto-detecting the
    live part each time via current_dasm_cpu(), unchanged) -- skips (77)
    without system ROMs, like debug_test and the video tests.

    core/debugger/m6809dasm.c's HD6309 support is a second, complete opcode
    table (hd6309_instructions[3][256], mechanically extracted from
    src/mc6809/hd6309_trace.c the same way the original 6809 table was from
    mc6809_trace.c -- about 40% of the 768 slots differ, so a verbatim
    transplant stays re-extractable without hand-merging a diff) plus every
    addressing mode the 6809 does not have: INMEM_DIRECT/INDEXED/EXTENDED
    (OIM/AIM/EIM/TIM), MEMBIT (the B{AND,OR,EOR} family), QUAD_IMMEDIATE
    (LDQ), the four TFM postbyte variants, a 16-entry TFR/EXG register table
    (the 6809's leaves six slots undefined), and three new indexed
    accumulator offsets (E/F/W) filling postbyte nibbles the 6809 leaves
    illegal -- plus a handful of full-postbyte values HD6309 hijacks
    specifically to mean ",W" addressing. coco_dasm_one_cpu() takes an
    explicit coco_dasm_cpu now; coco_dasm_one() is a thin 6809-default
    wrapper so existing callers are unchanged. The property the whole
    two-table design exists for -- a byte pattern only valid on one chip
    must decode as illegal on the other, not as "whatever either chip
    supports" -- has its own dasm_test cases, and the existing 65536-opening
    sweep now runs for both chips.

    The memory pane is a 16x16 hex+ASCII dump under the disassembly/register
    row, with an address entry and Go button (GtkEntry / QLineEdit) to jump
    around; same poll-and-redraw shape as the rest of the window, built on
    the already-tested cocodebug_read. Verified by hand in both frontends via
    COCO_OPEN_DEBUGGER=1 (window.c / MainWindow.cpp's developer affordance
    that opens the debugger alongside the main window at startup).

    core/debugger/cocovideo.c decodes the screen from scratch -- alpha/
    SG4/SG6/CG/RG for a real CoCo 1/2 (VDG + SAM), plus a CoCo 3's GIME in
    both its VDG-compatible and native text/graphics modes -- entirely
    independent of XRoar's own live renderer, since none of that renderer's
    mode state is exposed publicly. Two things had no other way to reach:
      * GIME's video mode/palette-selector registers are, per real GIME
        hardware, write-only even through the CPU's own bus, and XRoar's
        TCC1014 keeps them in a private struct with no getter. Fixed with a
        small read-only tcc1014_get_debug_state() added to the *vendored*
        XRoar source by tools/xroar/patch-staged-tree.py (job 3), the same
        way that script already patches src/ui.c and src/ao.c -- so it
        survives a re-stage instead of being lost.
      * GIME fetches video RAM directly from physical memory, bypassing the
        CPU's MMU entirely -- a 19-bit video address is not a CPU-visible
        16-bit one, and reading it via cocodebug_read (the normal, MMU-aware
        CPU bus path) silently returns the wrong bytes once the MMU state
        diverges from the hardwired identity mapping XRoar resets into. No
        patch needed for this one: struct ram (ram.h) and its ram_a8() are
        already public, so gime_vram_read() reaches physical RAM directly,
        the same way coco3.c's own fetch_vram() does.
    Both bugs were invisible to a plain "is the screen not one flat colour"
    check and were only caught by dumping the decoded buffer to a PPM and
    looking at it -- video_test's two ctest cases (video_coco3, video_coco2)
    keep the flat-colour check as a cheap regression guard, but a real
    content change needs eyes on a real frame.
    GIME's *native* mode (COCO=0) is implemented from the same register map
    but has no automated or by-hand visual verification yet -- the default
    boot state never reaches it (GIME resets into VDG-compatible mode) -- so
    treat it as less trusted than the other three paths until something
    exercises it.

M7 (done) CI: linux, macOS, Windows and flatpak.
    .github/workflows/linux.yml: configure/build/ctest with WITH_COCO_ROMS=OFF
    (the system ROMs are not redistributable, so CI never has them -- boot_
    smoke, drivewire_smoke, debug and the two video_coco* tests correctly
    report ctest's skip code there, the same as any WITH_COCO_ROMS=OFF build).
    SDL3 is built from source and pinned to release-3.4.12 rather than
    assumed to have an apt package on the runner image -- SDL3 is too new for
    that to be a safe assumption, and this is the same "pin what matters"
    policy as XRoar's SHA256 tarball and the FujiNet firmware's pinned
    commit. Verified locally: a from-scratch WITH_COCO_ROMS=OFF configure
    (using the system package manager's SDL3, not a from-source build -- that
    exact recipe was already proven working on this machine, just not
    re-run start to finish for this) builds both frontends and ctest greens
    the 10 tests that run, skipping the 5 ROM-gated ones as expected.
    Verified on GitHub Actions too (run 30384781655, ubuntu-latest): green
    end to end after one fix -- libxtst-dev was missing, SDL3's X11 build
    needs it for the XTEST extension and fails configure without it.

    Also fixed while validating this: third_party/fujinet-firmware/ (the
    default clone location when FUJINET_SRC is not set to point at an
    existing checkout) was not in .gitignore -- only third_party/xroar/ was.
    A plain from-scratch `cmake -B build` -- exactly what CI does -- would
    have left an untracked 3700+ file clone sitting in the working tree.

    .github/workflows/macos.yml: same shape, macos-latest. No macOS frontend
    exists yet (M10), so this builds core + the FujiNet runtime + the test
    suite only -- add_frontend() skips a frontend directory with no
    CMakeLists.txt rather than failing the configure -- which still
    exercises everything M6 touched on a second OS and compiler (Apple
    clang) ahead of M10 actually needing a working macOS build. WITH_FUJINET
    left at its default (ON): tools/fujinet/build-fujinet-desktop.sh already
    has Darwin-specific handling (libfujinet.dylib, and it always builds
    mbedTLS 3.6.5 from source on Darwin rather than trusting a system copy),
    so this was meant to prove that out rather than skip it. Green on the
    first run (30751731520) with no fixes needed -- confirmed from the log
    that the FujiNet runtime actually built (mbedTLS included) rather than
    silently no-op'ing, and that all 11 tests ran or skipped as expected.

    .github/workflows/windows.yml: native windows-latest + msys2/setup-
    msys2 (UCRT64), not the mingw-w64 cross-compile documented in cmake/
    toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake -- that recipe exists only because the
    maintainer has no Windows machine to build on directly; this workflow is
    what actually ships in CI/CD, so it drives the real native toolchain,
    matching how build-fujinet-desktop.sh already expected to run (its own
    MSYS2/cygpath handling). Same core-only scope as macos.yml (no Windows
    frontend yet). MSYS2's UCRT64 repo happens to package the exact SDL3 pin
    (3.4.12), so this uses that directly rather than building from source,
    with a step that fails loudly if it ever drifts from the pin.

    Took seven pushes to go green (30753091420), each diagnosed locally
    first against the mingw-w64 cross toolchain already in this repo
    (cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake) rather than by round-tripping through
    CI -- faster, and it meant every push after the first had already been
    proven to fix the specific error the previous run reported:
      * config.h never defined WINDOWS32 -- the macro src/becker.c and 17
        other unmodified upstream files branch on for basic POSIX-vs-
        Winsock differences, not _WIN32 (which this project's own generated
        config.h uses everywhere else). Autotools' configure would normally
        define it; nothing here was doing that job. Fixed in
        generate_config_h().
      * HAVE_STRSEP was unconditionally 1 on the reasoning that portalib
        only needs to supply strsep() "when the host lacks it" -- true for
        glibc/Darwin (both behind _BSD_SOURCE, which src/joystick.c
        defines), false for MinGW-w64. Moved behind !defined(_WIN32).
      * Once WINDOWS32 was defined, xroar.c pulled in the native win32 GUI
        module's header (windows32/common_windows32.h, which wants SDL2)
        and called windows32_init()/windows32_shutdown() -- upstream only
        builds these alongside ui_windows32, which nothing here registers
        (our own ui_desktop_module stands in for it, like every other
        frontend). A first attempt excluded ALL of xroar.c's WINDOWS32
        branches with one #undef; that broke the mkdir() branch too (a
        genuine, correct POSIX-vs-Windows difference elsewhere in the same
        file) and CI caught it immediately. Replaced with six precise
        per-site "&& !defined(XROAR_DESKTOP_MODULES)" guards instead, plus
        three more of the same shape once linking exposed src/ui.c's
        filereq_windows32_module/ui_windows32_module module-list entries
        and src/hkbd.c's hk_windows_update_keymap() (an optional keymap
        auto-detection enhancement on the same #elif ladder as HAVE_X11/
        HAVE_COCOA, neither of which this project defines either -- Windows
        now takes the same hk_default_update_keymap() fallback Linux and
        macOS already did).
      * core/tests/session_test.c called _mkdir() under #if defined(_WIN32)
        without #include <direct.h> (boot_smoke.c already had this right).
      * fujinet-firmware's own build wants plain `make`, not the
        mingw32-make.exe the mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-make package provides --
        installed MSYS2's own base `make` instead.
    All nine fixes are in tools/xroar/patch-staged-tree.py (the config.h/
    xroar.c/ui.c/hkbd.c ones), core/tests/session_test.c, and windows.yml
    itself; none are Windows-specific hacks bolted on top -- they teach the
    shared staging/generation step what MinGW-w64 actually needs, the same
    place every other cross-platform difference already lives.

    build-aux/flatpak/online.fujinet.go.coco.{gnome,kde}.yml + .github/
    workflows/flatpak.yml: two self-contained manifests (GNOME on
    org.gnome.Platform//49, KDE on org.kde.Platform//6.11 -- KDE drops the
    embedded web view, QtWebEngine's whole-Chromium base app being a poor
    trade for the CONFIG page a browser tab already opens fine via the
    OpenURI portal), adapted from fujinet-go-adam-desktop's proven pattern
    (same sdl3/mbedtls/python3-webui-deps modules; CoCo has no equivalent to
    adamcore, so the main module's sources are just this tree plus the
    pinned XRoar tarball -- as a flatpak `type: archive` source, exactly
    what cmake/Dependencies.cmake's own comment already anticipated -- and
    the pinned fujinet-firmware commit, kept in step with
    cmake/Dependencies.cmake by hand). `-DWITH_COCO_ROMS=OFF` is baked into
    both manifests, per COMPLIANCE.md.

    Built and installed locally with flatpak-builder (both runtimes and
    SDKs were already present on this machine) on the first try for both
    manifests, no fixes needed; `flatpak run` on each booted the emulator,
    correctly reported the CoCo 3 ROM slot unpopulated (WITH_COCO_ROMS=OFF
    doing its job), and brought FujiNet up over the Becker port exactly like
    the native builds do.

    The CI job (matrix over both manifests) does NOT use flathub-infra's
    prebuilt gnome-NN/kde-N.NN build containers: at the time this was
    written that image set only went up to kde-6.10, one version behind
    what this project is actually pinned to and already runs locally, so
    the workflow installs plain `flatpak`/`flatpak-builder` and pulls the
    runtime + SDK straight from Flathub instead -- slower, no cross-run
    cache, but it is exactly the recipe already verified locally rather
    than a second untested path.

M8 (done) Icons, desktop entries, AppStream metainfo, screenshots.
    Desktop entries and metainfo exist for both Linux frontends. Icon art
    too: tools/icons/make-icons.py composites fujinet-go-coco's (the Android
    app's) xxxhdpi adaptive icon layers -- background + foreground, already
    safe-zone padded -- into the same circular badge its flattened
    ic_launcher.png uses, then renders every hicolor size CMakeLists.txt
    installs (16..512) into data/icons/hicolor/. It is a one-shot dev tool
    (takes the Android checkout's res/ dir as an argument) rather than a
    build step, since the source Android checkout is not expected to be
    present for everyone building this repo; the rendered PNGs are committed.

    data/screenshots/1_fujinet_config.png and 2_debugger.png (both metainfo
    files reference the same two, since the app looks the same either
    frontend): real captures, not mockups -- the GNOME frontend built with
    system ROMs already present on this machine, launched on a live
    Hyprland session, grabbed with grim against the actual window geometry
    (hyprctl clients), then cropped to trim the emulator canvas's own
    letterboxing rather than resized, so what ships is pixel-for-pixel what
    the app renders. #1 is HDB-DOS's auto-boot into FujiNet CONFIG; #2 is
    the debugger (COCO_OPEN_DEBUGGER=1) mid-boot, disassembly/registers/
    memory panes and the Load Symbols…/Clear Symbols buttons all visible.

M9 (done) Packaging + release job.
    No sibling port has a CPack or release-workflow precedent to copy (adam-
    desktop's own "Cutting a release" ships plain zips + flatpak, no CPack at
    all), so this is new: CPack in the top-level CMakeLists.txt, one package
    per frontend rather than one package for both -- GNOME (GTK4/libadwaita)
    and KDE (Qt6) pull in entirely different runtime library stacks, so
    CPACK_PACKAGE_NAME switches on the FRONTEND cache variable
    (fujinet-go-coco-gnome / -kde / plain fujinet-go-coco for an FRONTEND=all
    dev build) rather than trying to split one build into components.
    DEB/RPM dependencies are auto-detected (CPACK_DEBIAN_PACKAGE_SHLIBDEPS,
    CPACK_RPM_PACKAGE_AUTOREQPROV) instead of hand-listed, since a fixed list
    goes stale the moment a dependency's package name changes across
    distributions and GTK vs Qt alone makes one list wrong for whichever
    frontend was not built. Added LICENSE (verbatim GPL-3.0 text, copied from
    fujinet-go-adam-desktop's -- same licence, not project-specific content)
    for CPACK_RESOURCE_FILE_LICENSE. While in there: the icon-install loop
    used to install both frontends' hicolor icon sets regardless of which
    was actually built (harmless when nothing referenced the orphaned one,
    but a packaged archive should not ship files for an app it does not
    contain) -- tightened to install only for a TARGET that exists.

    .github/workflows/release.yml, triggered on a `v*` tag: a check-version
    job that fails the whole run before anything builds if the tag,
    CMakeLists.txt's project() VERSION and both frontends' metainfo
    <release version="..."> entries disagree (same discipline as adam-
    desktop's release job, adapted since there is no macOS/Windows leg
    yet); a linux-packages matrix (gnome, kde) that configures with
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr, builds, runs ctest, then `cpack -G
    "DEB;RPM;TGZ"`; a flatpaks matrix building both manifests and
    `flatpak build-bundle`-ing each into a single installable file (same
    runtime-pull-from-Flathub approach as flatpak.yml, for the same reason:
    no flathub-infra prebuilt image existed yet for kde-6.11 when this was
    written); and a release job that downloads every artifact and creates
    (or updates) a draft GitHub Release via `gh release create`/`upload`.
    "Cutting a release" in the README documents the actual steps and lists
    the assets this produces today; macOS/Windows rows join that table once
    M10 lands.

    Verified locally as far as this machine can: `cpack -G TGZ` for a
    FRONTEND=gnome build (dpkg-deb and rpmbuild are not installed here,
    Arch Linux ships neither by default) produces a correctly laid-out
    archive -- binary, desktop entry, metainfo, hicolor icons for that
    frontend's app id only, the FujiNet runtime tree -- and the icon-loop
    tightening confirmed by diffing the tarball's contents before and
    after. DEB/RPM generation and the release workflow's own YAML have not
    been run end to end (that needs an actual `v*` tag push); the version-
    check and metainfo-grep logic were tested against this tree's real
    files by hand, not just read.

M10 (part) macOS and Windows frontends, remaining debugger windows, polish.

    Windows frontend done: frontends/windows/{main.c,resource.h,resource.rc,
    app.manifest.in,app.ico,debugger/dbg_window.{c,h}}, native Win32/GDI, no
    toolkit -- matching the family's own convention (see
    fujinet-go-adam-desktop/frontends/windows/, the structural reference).
    Not a line-for-line port: several of ADAM's assumptions do not hold for
    CoCo --
      * cocodebug is poll-only (cocodebug_stop_serial(), compared on the
        existing 100ms WM_TIMER), not adamdebug's stop-callback-plus-
        WM_DBG_STOPPED-marshaling -- XRoar's instruction-hook context makes
        a cross-thread callback into a toolkit unsafe, the same reason the
        GNOME/KDE debuggers poll too.
      * No register or memory *editing* API exists (cocodebug_regs_get has
        no _set, cocodebug_read has no _write) -- display-only, matching
        GNOME.
      * No VDP, no instruction trace (ADAM's debugger has both, CoCo has
        neither) -- replaced with one decoded-screen pixel pane
        (cocodebug_video_decode) and Load Symbols…/Clear Symbols buttons
        (cocodebug_symbols_load/_clear -- new UI, ADAM's own symbol tables
        load automatically with no frontend affordance to copy). No tab
        control at all: with VDP and Trace gone there is nothing left to
        justify one, so the debugger window is a single flattened CPU+
        Video layout instead of ADAM's three-tab one.
      * cocosession_copy_frame's frame size is dynamic (up to 800x300, the
        video mode can change at runtime), not a compile-time constant like
        ADAM's 256x212 -- the present thread and dest_rect()/BITMAPINFO
        track g_fb_w/g_fb_h per frame. Its XRGB8888 pixels are already
        GDI's BI_RGB 32bpp byte order on a little-endian host (same
        conclusion GNOME's own code comment draws for its GDK format), so
        neither the main display nor the debugger's video pane need the
        RGB565->BGRA conversion loop ADAM's present thread has.
      * Keyboard input is forwarded directly via cocosession_key(down,
        keysym, unicode, ctrl, shift) per keystroke rather than ADAM's
        single pre-translated byte. Win32 VK codes carry no shift state of
        their own, so rather than reconstruct a shifted symbol with
        ToUnicode (and have to keep key-down/key-up consistent despite
        transient modifier state), non-special keys resolve straight to
        their base US-layout character -- stable across down/up by
        construction, and exactly the "unshifted symbol on the same
        physical key" core/src/input_map.c's coco_key_from_event already
        wants. ADAM's Ctrl+digit ColecoVision keypad-as-joystick trick has
        no CoCo equivalent and is dropped, not translated.
      * CoCo's live Machine/TV-input/Artifact-Colour/CPU menus (no ADAM
        equivalent at all) are built at runtime by walking each
        cocosession_*_name(idx) table until NULL -- the same shape as
        GNOME's radio_menu() helper -- into four IDM_*_BASE-relative
        command-id ranges, with CheckMenuRadioItem for the check marks
        (Win32 has no stateful action objects) and the whole menu bar
        rebuilt after a machine switch so Display only offers the TV inputs
        the new machine actually has (RGB disappears on a CoCo 2).
      * cocosession_import_rom/has_system_roms (CoCo's ROMs are not
        embedded, unlike ADAM's) needed their own Import System ROMs...
        menu item -- GetOpenFileNameA with OFN_ALLOWMULTISELECT, mirroring
        GNOME's action_import_roms.
    Fixed one latent bug found while adapting rather than carried over: a
    line-for-line port of ADAM's fullscreen toggle never reattaches the
    menu bar on restore (SetMenu is only ever called to remove it); CoCo's
    version calls SetMenu(hwnd, g_menu) again when leaving fullscreen. Also
    fixed vs. a naive port: the debugger's WM_CLOSE (which hides rather
    than destroys, so F12 reopens instantly) calls
    cocodebug_set_engaged(d->dbg, 0) right there rather than deferring
    cleanup to WM_DESTROY the way ADAM defers clearing its stop callback --
    WM_DESTROY with this hide-not-destroy shape only fires at process exit,
    so deferring would leave the emulator running the slow instruction-
    hooked path forever every time the window is merely closed.

    Verified locally as far as this machine can: both new .c files compile
    clean (zero warnings) against the mingw-w64 cross toolchain already in
    this repo, and every cocosession_*/cocodebug_*/coco_debugger_* symbol
    they reference resolves against the real headers (checked by hand,
    function by function). A full link was not possible here -- MSYS2's
    prebuilt libtre/libsystre packages are what CI's windows.yml installs,
    and this dev machine has neither pre-built nor a quick path to one
    (TRE is autotools upstream, not the CMake build the toolchain file's
    own SDL3/mbedTLS recipes use; a from-source build was started but not
    finished once it became clear the packages CI already has solve this).
    The ordinary Linux build and full ctest suite are unaffected
    (frontends/windows/CMakeLists.txt returns immediately on a non-WIN32
    configure). Verified on GitHub Actions (run 30756620974): green on the
    first push, no iteration needed -- fujinet-go-coco-windows.exe actually
    linked and no_embedded_roms_fujinet-go-coco-windows ran against it and
    passed, confirmed from the log rather than assumed.

    Settings window added afterwards (closing the gap this entry originally
    flagged), plain Win32 controls (a combobox + checkboxes on a small
    top-level window, no dialog-resource template) built the same way the
    debugger window's controls are: Aspect ratio/Smooth scaling apply live,
    FujiNet/Sound/Gamepads are collected and applied by restarting the
    session on WM_DESTROY, matching GNOME's Preferences dialog keys and
    defaults (frontends/gnome/prefs.c) so a machine configured in one
    frontend shows up the same in another.

    Same pass also caught and fixed a real cross-frontend bug: dest_rect's
    aspect_mode values had been ported straight from ADAM's own convention
    (0=native, 1=TV 4:3), but GNOME's actual CocoAspectMode
    (frontends/gnome/display.h) is 0=TV 4:3, 1=square pixels -- backwards
    for two of the three values. Fixed in both this file and the macOS
    DisplayView.m at the same time (found while building the Settings
    window's aspect-ratio combo, whose item order needed to match).

    macOS frontend done: frontends/macos/{main.m,AppDelegate.{h,m},
    DisplayView.{h,m},debugger/DebuggerWindow.{h,m},Info.plist.in,
    CMakeLists.txt} + data/icons/fujinet-go-coco.icns, native AppKit
    (Objective-C), no toolkit -- same convention, fujinet-go-adam-desktop/
    frontends/macos/ the structural reference. The same divergences as
    Windows apply here (poll-only cocodebug, no register/memory editing,
    no VDP/trace -> one video pane + Load Symbols…/Clear Symbols, dynamic
    frame size, live Machine/TV-input/Artifact-Colour/CPU radio menus with
    no ADAM equivalent) -- see the Windows entry above for the reasoning,
    which was written once and applies to both ports rather than repeated
    here. macOS-specific points:
      * NSEvent.charactersIgnoringModifiers already gives a layout-aware
        *unshifted* character, unlike Win32's positional VK codes with no
        shift state -- so unlike Windows this needed no ToUnicode-
        avoidance workaround, ADAM's existing function-key-to-X11-keysym
        table ports essentially unchanged, and keyUp: forwards a real
        release (cocosession_key wants both edges; ADAM's ColecoVision
        keypad hack it also had here is dropped, same as Windows).
      * The XRGB8888-already / no-conversion-loop point applies to
        CGImageCreate too: kCGImageAlphaNoneSkipFirst |
        kCGBitmapByteOrder32Little is the BGRX-in-memory flag combination,
        replacing ADAM's alpha-last RGBA flags and manual RGB565
        conversion, for both the main display and the debugger's video
        pane.
      * Live radio menus use NSMenuItem.state/.tag directly (AppKit menu
        items track their own checked state; no CheckMenuRadioItem
        equivalent needed) and the whole bar is rebuilt after any change,
        same simplification already used for Windows/GNOME.
      * Toggle Full Screen is the stock toggleFullScreen: action --
        genuinely simpler than Windows' manual WS_OVERLAPPEDWINDOW/
        WINDOWPLACEMENT dance, no custom fullscreen code at all.
      * FujiNet Configuration keeps the embedded WKWebView (unlike
        Windows' external-browser-only choice): WebKit.framework ships
        with macOS at zero bundle-size cost, so the KDE-vs-GNOME size
        trade-off that made Windows skip it does not apply here.
      * Settings window (done, closing the gap this entry originally
        flagged): a scaled-down port of ADAM's own NSGridView-based
        showSettings: -- Aspect ratio/Smooth scaling (live) and FujiNet/
        Sound/Gamepads (collected, applied by restarting the session in
        windowWillClose:, same as ADAM's _machineDirty pattern) -- with
        ADAM's machine/palette/expansion/joystick-mode/keypad rows dropped
        entirely (no CoCo equivalent) and Machine/TV input/Artifact
        Colour/CPU not duplicated (already live in the main menu). ⌘, in
        the App menu, matching ADAM's placement and Mac convention.
      * Same debugger WM_CLOSE-equivalent fix as Windows:
        windowShouldClose: calls cocodebug_set_engaged(_dbg, 0) before
        hiding (returns NO, keeping the window alive for F12 to reshow),
        rather than only at process exit the way ADAM's stop-callback
        teardown effectively would with the same hide-not-destroy shape.

    Verification asymmetry vs. Windows: there is no macOS SDK or cross
    toolchain on this Linux machine (unlike mingw-w64 for Windows), so no
    local compile check was possible at all -- clang -fsyntax-only cannot
    even resolve <Cocoa/Cocoa.h>, let alone validate AppKit API usage.
    Every method name/signature was instead cross-referenced by hand
    against ADAM's own already-shipped, working source (read in full this
    session) rather than from memory. One real bug this care caught before
    it shipped: an NSOpenPanel.allowedContentTypes set to an empty array
    (meant as "no restriction" for the Load Symbols dialog) would actually
    have allowed nothing -- fixed by leaving the property unset instead.
    Push-and-watch-CI is the only verification this pass got; expect this
    loop to matter more here than it did for Windows, which had the mingw
    pre-check to lean on. CI packaging depth is intentionally the same as
    Windows' too: .github/workflows/macos.yml is unmodified (still builds
    SDL3 shared into a scratch prefix, fine for CI validation), and the
    static-SDL3/otool-L-sweep/code-signing/notarization/release.yml-
    asset-list work fujinet-go-adam-desktop's own macOS job does is
    explicitly deferred, not silently dropped.


Known issues / things to remember
---------------------------------

* src/path.c:194 hardcodes ':' as the rompath separator. On Windows that
  collides with a drive letter. Deal with it at M10 (or send a fix upstream).
* MinGW-w64 has no regex.h and portalib/sdsx.c needs one, reached from
  src/path.c and src/xconfig.c. config.h already defines HAVE_TRE on _WIN32
  and core/CMakeLists.txt links systre/tre; the Windows toolchain has to
  provide them (see cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake).
* Restarting a session calls xroar_shutdown() and then xroar_init() again.
  The Android app never does this (it kills the process), so it is untested.
  If it turns out XRoar cannot be re-initialised in one process, the fix is to
  make the session's restart path rebuild the machine in place instead --
  cocohost_set_machine() already does exactly that without re-init.
* prefs.c casts g_free to GClosureNotify and gcc warns. Carried over from the
  Apple II port, where it warns identically; it is a GLib API shape, not a
  bug here.
